var tafsirData_28 = {
  "1": "\"Ta-Sin-Mim\" opens this new chapter as \"Ta-Sin\" opened the chapter before it. These are among the disconnected letters, \"huruf muqatta'ah,\" that begin several surahs. The Prophet recited them as letters. The Companions received them as part of the Quran. Reliable scholarship has not locked a single worldly meaning for them. Some said they are names of the surah, some said they are an oath, some said they point to the fact that the Book is made of the same letters the Arabs know, yet they cannot match it. The safest speech is that their full meaning is with Allah, and that they still do a clear work: they stop the listener at the door of a revelation that is not ordinary talk.\n\nThe extra letter \"Mim\" beside \"Ta-Sin\" marks this chapter as kin to the last and not a copy of it. Both will speak of Moses. This one will linger on his infancy, his mistake in the city, his flight, his hiring in Midian, and his standing before Pharaoh with Aaron. The letters are the threshold of that longer narration.\n\nBeginning with letters also resists the charge of \"ancient fables.\" A fable starts with \"once there was.\" This Book starts with sounds that belong to the Quran's own signature. Then it will say, these are the verses of the clear Book. The letters and the claim of clarity stand together. What begins in a way humans do not invent continues as a story told \"in truth for people who believe.\"\n\nNo Arabic script is needed to hold the letters. Their recited names are enough. The believer reads them, trusts them, and steps into the verses that follow with the same trust. That is already a small act of the submission the previous chapter commanded.",
  "2": "\"These are the verses of the clear Book.\" The same kind of sentence followed the letters in the previous chapter. \"These\" points to what is being recited. \"Verses\" are \"ayat,\" signs as well as sentences. \"The clear Book,\" \"al-kitab al-mubin,\" is the Quran as a writing that makes things plain.\n\nClarity here does not mean that every secret of the unseen is dated. The last chapter said none knows the unseen except Allah. Clarity means the guidance is not riddled on purpose. Commands, stories, and arguments can be understood by people who believe and think.\n\nRepeating this line after another set of letters teaches that each surah shares the same source and the same quality. A new chapter is not a new religion. It is more of the clear Book. The coming story of Moses and Pharaoh is therefore not entertainment. It is verses, signs, part of the Book that clarifies.\n\nMecca accused the Prophet of copying storytellers. A storyteller does not introduce his night-tale as \"verses of the clear Book.\" That title makes a claim of revelation and of moral transparency. The next verse will state the claim even more tightly: We narrate to you part of the story of Moses and Pharaoh in truth for people who believe. The clear Book is about to prove its clarity by telling a known history with a purpose.",
  "3": "\"We narrate to you, O Prophet, part of the story of Moses and Pharaoh in truth.\" Allah is the narrator. The Prophet is the addressee. The story is only \"part,\" \"min naba',\" not every detail of those long years. What is told is enough for the lesson. \"In truth\" answers the charge of fables. This is \"bil-haqq,\" with truth, not with legend-padding.\n\n\"For people who believe\" names the first audience who benefit, as the previous chapter said the Book is a guide for believers. Others may hear the same names, Moses and Pharaoh, and still deny. Believers hear a pattern of their own time: a tyrant, an oppressed people, a threatened infant, a promise of Allah, a messenger.\n\nThe previous chapter already told Moses at the fire, the staff, the hand, and Pharaoh's arrogant denial. This chapter goes back to the beginning of that conflict in the land of Egypt. The two tellings are not rivals. They are parts. Together they give a fuller naba'.\n\n\"We narrate\" also comforts a Prophet who was told only to recite and warn. Recitation includes these narrations. He does not have to invent a reply to every political cruelty in Mecca. Allah will set Pharaoh on the page so that Quraysh can see their own features if they wish, and so that oppressed believers can see that Allah once willed to favor the weak.\n\nThe next verse begins the truth of the story with Pharaoh's method: he elevated himself, divided people, slaughtered sons, and kept women. The narration does not start with a miracle. It starts with a political crime. Truth includes that crime.",
  "4": "\"Indeed, Pharaoh arrogantly elevated himself in the land\" is the first stroke of the tyrant's portrait. \"Ala\" means he rose up, exalted himself, acted high. The land is Egypt, the place of his throne. Height here is not the height of a mountain Allah placed. It is the false height of a man who made himself the measure of the earth.\n\n\"And divided its people into subservient groups\" shows the method of that height. He did not only boast. He cut the population into parties, \"shiya'an,\" so that some could be used against others and one group could be crushed. Divide and weaken is an old tool. The Quran names it as Pharaoh's work.\n\n\"One of which he persecuted, slaughtering their sons and keeping their women.\" The targeted group is the Children of Israel. Sons are killed, daughters or women are left. Reports in tafsir connect this to Pharaoh's fear of a child who would end his rule, a fear the next verses will turn into irony when that child is raised in his house. The verse itself stresses the cruelty: slaughter of boys, retention of women, a policy against a people's future.\n\n\"He was truly one of the corruptors.\" The title \"mufsidin\" ties him to the corruptors of the last chapter whose end the listener was told to see. Corruption is not only private vice. It is this public breaking of a land and a people.\n\nThe believers in Mecca, some of them weak and persecuted, hear that Allah's clear Book begins the story by siding with the naming of oppression. Before Moses speaks, the regime is already judged. The next verse will say what Allah willed for the oppressed: favor, models of faith, and succession.",
  "5": "\"But it was Our Will to favour those who were oppressed in the land\" turns the whole height of Pharaoh into a setup for Allah's will. \"Nuridu,\" We will, We intend. The oppressed, \"alladhina istud'ifu,\" are those made weak, the same Israel Pharaoh slaughtered. Favor, \"mann,\" is a gracious bestowal, not a wage they could force.\n\n\"Making them models of faith as well as successors\" names two gifts inside the favor. Imams, leaders and examples, and \"warithin,\" inheritors or successors in the land. The people who were cut into a hunted group will become the ones followed and the ones who remain after the tyrant.\n\nThis will is stated before the baby is placed in the river. The listener is not left to wonder whether the slaughter will be the last word. Allah announces the end at the beginning, as He announced to the mockers that He is never unaware.\n\nA misconception would be to read the verse as a promise that every oppressed group in history will automatically become rulers. The verse is about this people and this will of Allah, in a story told in truth. The wider lesson is that oppression does not bind Allah's hands, and that He may choose the weak to inherit. It is not a blank check for every political slogan.\n\nThe previous verse's corruptor and this verse's oppressed are a pair. Human division and slaughter meet a divine intention that runs the other way. The next verse will add establishment in the land and a showing to Pharaoh, Haman, and the soldiers of the very thing they feared.",
  "6": "\"And to establish them in the land\" continues the objects of Allah's will. Establishment, \"numakkina,\" is firm footing, authority, a place that is theirs after being hunted. The oppressed will not only escape. They will be settled.\n\n\"And through them show Pharaoh, Haman, and their soldiers the fulfilment of what they feared.\" Haman appears here as Pharaoh's ally in the crime, named with him and with the armies. What they feared was the rise of Israel, the loss of control, the child of the vision or the growing strength of a subject people. Allah willed that the fear would come true through the very people they tried to erase.\n\nThe irony is already in motion. Killing sons was meant to stop the fear. The surviving plan of Allah will use a surviving son in Pharaoh's own palace. The soldiers who carried out policy will see, in the end, the opposite of the policy's goal.\n\nNaming Haman and the soldiers widens blame beyond one king. A tyrant's house includes ministers and troops. The Quran does not let the machinery of persecution hide behind the throne.\n\n\"What they feared\" also speaks to every regime that rules by panic. Fear can drive slaughter. It cannot outrun Allah's showing. The next verse leaves the palace strategy and enters a mother's room. The will that favors the oppressed begins with inspiration to a nursing woman, a river, and a promise that the baby will return and become a messenger. The large political will is about to take a small papyrus ark.",
  "7": "\"We inspired the mother of Moses\" brings the grand will down to a woman's heart. \"Awhayna\" is inspiration, a true directing from Allah. She is not called a prophet in the way Moses will be, but she is spoken to with a clear plan. The oppressed people's rescue starts with a mother.\n\n\"Nurse him, but when you fear for him, put him then into the river.\" First, feed him. The bond of milk is allowed and commanded. Then, when the slaughterer's fear arrives at her door, place him in the river, the Nile. The command sounds impossible. A river is death to an infant unless Allah is the commander.\n\n\"And do not fear or grieve.\" Two feelings are forbidden to rule her, as two feelings were forbidden to rule the Prophet in the last chapter: grief and distress. She will feel. She must not drown in the feeling. The next words give the reason she can obey.\n\n\"We will certainly return him to you, and make him one of the messengers.\" Two promises: return, and messengerhood. The baby in the box is already given a future beyond survival. The mother is asked to trust a plan that includes prophecy, not only hiding.\n\nThis is how Allah's will to favor the oppressed looks at ground level: a nursing command, a river command, a ban on fear and grief, and a double promise. The story of the queen of Sheba showed a woman reasoning about a letter. This story shows a woman obeying an inspiration that cuts against every maternal instinct except the deepest one, trust that the child is safer in Allah's river than in a hunted house. The next verse will show Pharaoh's people picking him up, so that the enemy becomes the foster home.",
  "8": "\"And it so happened that Pharaoh's people picked him up, only to become their enemy and source of grief.\" The box reaches the house of the killer, not a random fisherman only. \"Li-yakuna lahum 'aduwwan wa hazanan,\" so that he would be an enemy and a sorrow for them. They thought they were picking up a foundling. They were picking up their future grief.\n\n\"Surely Pharaoh, Haman, and their soldiers were sinful.\" The same three are named again. Their sin is the soil in which this irony grows. Because they were \"khat'in,\" sinful, the child they lift will not become a loyal Egyptian prince in the end. He will become the messenger who faces them.\n\nThe verse teaches that Allah's plan can use the tyrant's own gate. No need for the baby to vanish to a far mountain first. The palace that slaughtered sons now funds the life of the son who matters most to that story.\n\n\"Enemy and source of grief\" is said before Moses grows or strikes anyone. The end is spoken in the beginning, like the will to favor the oppressed. Listeners who fear that evil systems are closed should hear this. The system imported its own undoing and called it charity or chance.\n\nThe next verse will give Pharaoh's wife the words that save the baby's life inside that palace: a source of joy, do not kill him, perhaps he will be useful or we will adopt him. They pick him up. She speaks. They remain unaware. The grief is postponed, but it is already decreed.",
  "9": "\"Pharaoh's wife said to him, 'This baby is a source of joy for me and you.'\" A voice of mercy speaks inside the house of slaughter. She calls the child \"qurrat 'ayn,\" coolness of the eye, a joy that rests the gaze. She includes Pharaoh, \"for me and you,\" trying to tie the baby's life to the king's own pleasure.\n\n\"Do not kill him.\" The policy of killing Israelite boys meets a wife's plea. Whether Pharaoh had already moved to apply the policy to this foundling or the danger was obvious, her word is a shield. The Quran elsewhere names her among the righteous who said, My Lord, build me a house with You in Paradise. Here she is the means of the promise, \"We will return him,\" taking its first public step.\n\n\"Perhaps he may be useful to us or we may adopt him as a son.\" She offers two worldly reasons a tyrant might accept: use, or adoption. She does not preach Moses' future prophethood. She speaks the language of the palace. Allah's plan can wear that language for a night.\n\n\"They were unaware of what was to come.\" The refrain of unawareness from Thamud's plotters returns. Pharaoh's house does not know the grief they have lifted from the water. Adoption, in their mind, is control. In Allah's will, it is positioning.\n\nSome reports name her Asiyah. The verse itself honors her act more than her name. In a land of divided groups and slaughtered sons, one woman said do not kill him. The clear Book records that sentence as part of the truth of Moses and Pharaoh. The next verse will leave the palace for the other woman, the mother whose heart is about to break.",
  "10": "\"And the heart of Moses' mother ached so much that she almost gave away his identity.\" Inspiration did not turn her into stone. \"Farighan\" in the wider reading of this story is a heart emptied of patience or emptied of everything but Moses. The English here says it ached so much she almost revealed him. The promise of return was real. The ache was also real. Faith does not always feel like calm.\n\n\"Had We not reassured her heart in order for her to have faith in Allah's promise.\" Allah binds her heart, \"rabatna 'ala qalbiha,\" ties it down, steadies it, so that she remains a believer in the promise just given: We will return him and make him a messenger. The purpose of the reassurance is faith, \"li-takuna min al-mu'minin.\"\n\nTwo women, two hearts: the wife's joy in the palace, the mother's ache outside. Allah uses both. He does not mock the ache. He treats it with a tying of the heart.\n\nThe Prophet who was told not to grieve can hear his own struggle in this mother. Revelation and human pain meet. The help is not the removal of all feeling. The help is a heart made able to keep faith.\n\n\"Almost gave away his identity\" shows how close the plan came to human collapse. The story is not a cartoon of perfect calm. It is a rescue that includes a nearly broken woman and a God who intervenes inside the chest. The next verse will show her still able to act: she tells the sister to follow, and the sister watches from a distance while the palace remains unaware.",
  "11": "\"And she said to his sister, 'Keep track of him!'\" The mother, heart now steadied enough to think, uses the family she has. The sister is told to follow, to trace the box and the child. Faith in the promise does not cancel ordinary means. It sends a girl to watch.\n\n\"So she watched him from a distance, while they were unaware.\" The sister becomes a scout. Distance protects her. Unawareness protects the mission. The palace that does not know the baby's future also does not know it is being watched by his blood.\n\nThe scene is tender and tense. A sister on the bank or in the streets of the compound, a baby inside, soldiers who kill boys, a queen who has claimed joy. The clear Book lets the listener stand with the sister's eyes.\n\nFamily teamwork under oppression is part of the narration in truth. The mother, the sister, and later the refusal of wet-nurses, all work together under Allah's arranging. No one of them owns the plan. Each does a part.\n\n\"Unaware\" is becoming a keyword of this opening: Pharaoh's house unaware, Thamud unaware in the last chapter, plotters unaware. Allah's friends watch. Allah's enemies do not see what matters. The next verse will show why the watching sister can speak up at the right moment: the baby refuses every nurse, so she can offer a household that will care for him, the household of his own mother.",
  "12": "\"And We had caused him to refuse all wet-nurses at first.\" The baby's mouth is under Allah's command. He will not take milk from the nurses of the palace. \"Harramna 'alayhi al-maradi'a\" is the Arabic behind this refusal: the wet-nurses were barred to him. The promise \"We will return him to you\" uses hunger as a servant.\n\n\"So his sister suggested, 'Shall I direct you to a family who will bring him up for you and take good care of him?'\" She does not blurt, that is my mother. She offers a service. The palace hears usefulness, the same note Pharaoh's wife had played. Care, \"yansahun,\" sincere looking-after, is what she promises.\n\nThe sister's intelligence is part of the inspiration's fruit. A clumsy word would have killed the child and the family. A careful offer opens the door. Allah's plan includes human tact.\n\nPharaoh's house, still unaware, will now pay the mother to nurse her own son. The oppressed woman will receive her child back with a wage from the slaughterer's treasury. The irony is almost too sharp, yet the Quran tells it as truth, not as a joke. It is the will to favor those who were oppressed, written in milk.\n\nThe next verse will state the purpose of this return: ease her heart, end her grief, and teach her that Allah's promise is always true, even though most people do not know.",
  "13": "\"This is how We returned him to his mother so that her heart would be put at ease, and not grieve.\" The first half of the river-promise is paid in this life, quickly. Return. The purposes are named: ease the heart, remove grief. The same woman told not to fear or grieve is now given the reason's fulfillment. Allah did not only command her feelings. He arranged the world so the feelings could rest.\n\n\"And that she would know that Allah's promise is always true.\" Knowledge is the third purpose. She already believed enough to obey. Now she knows by event. The promise was not a comfort-phrase. It was a report about the future that came due. \"Haqq\" true, as the story itself is told in truth.\n\n\"But most people do not know.\" The close widens. Most people do not know that Allah's promise is true, or do not know how this return happened, or do not know the hidden wisdom of fearful nights. The mother's knowledge is a privilege. The many live without that certainty.\n\nThe second half of the promise, \"make him one of the messengers,\" is still ahead. The mother is given enough of the truth to endure the wait for the rest. That is often how promises work in the Quran: a near fulfillment teaches trust for a farther one.\n\nMost people do not know, yet the clear Book now makes this knowing available to believers who hear. They are invited to treat Allah's other promises, including resurrection and the favoring of the oppressed in that ancient land, with the same seriousness this mother learned at a nursing breast. The next verse will leap years: full strength, maturity, wisdom, and knowledge as a reward for good-doers.",
  "14": "\"And when he reached full strength and maturity, We gave him wisdom and knowledge.\" The child of the river is now a man. \"Ashuddahu\" is full bodily strength. \"Istawa\" is evenness and maturity. At that stage Allah gives \"hukm\" and \"ilm,\" judgment and knowledge. The gifts are timed. Infancy had protection. Manhood receives a mind fit for a messenger, even before the fire in the valley that another chapter describes.\n\n\"This is how We reward the good-doers.\" Moses is counted among \"al-muhsinin,\" those who do good with excellence. The reward of wisdom and knowledge is not random talent. It is how Allah pays ihsan. The previous chapter said whoever comes with a good deed receives what is better. This verse shows a worldly form of that better: a sound mind and sound knowledge given to a man who will need both.\n\nThe leap over the palace years is deliberate. The Quran is not writing a full biography. It is narrating in truth the parts that teach. From return to the mother we jump to the grown man about to enter a city and make a terrible mistake. The gifts of wisdom and knowledge do not mean he will be incapable of a wrong punch. They mean Allah has prepared him, and the next trial will show his conscience.\n\nGood-doers in oppressed families should hear that Allah's reward may be inner equipment, not only a throne. Moses is not yet sent with the staff. He is already given what a just adult needs. The following verse will test that adult in an alley of two fighters, a punch, a death, and a cry that this is from Satan's handiwork.",
  "15": "\"One day he entered the city unnoticed by its people.\" The grown Moses moves in a city that does not mark his entrance. \"Ala hini ghaflah,\" in a time of unawareness. The keyword returns. He is not leading a parade. He is almost hidden.\n\n\"There he found two men fighting: one of his own people, and the other of his enemies.\" The divided land of Pharaoh appears as two bodies in a street. One is Israelite, \"his own people.\" One is from the enemy, the ruling people. The whole political crime of the opening verses is now a fistfight.\n\n\"The man from his people called to him for help against his foe. So Moses punched him, causing his death.\" Help is asked. Moses strikes. The blow kills. The Quran does not call this strike a prophetic miracle. It calls forth Moses' own verdict in the next breath.\n\n\"Moses cried, 'This is from Satan's handiwork. He is certainly a sworn, misleading enemy.'\" He names Satan, \"al-shaytan,\" as the source of the deed's evil. Satan is an enemy who swears to mislead. Moses does not say, I have struck a blow for freedom and it is purely good. He sees a dead man and reads the event as satanic corruption of the moment.\n\nScholars have discussed that Moses did not intend murder, that the punch was meant to stop injustice and landed too hard. The verse itself stresses the result and the repentance that follows. A coming messenger can still need forgiveness. The clear Book tells that truth so no one builds a false picture of prophets as men who never stumbled before their mission, or even in human error.\n\nThe next verse is the plea: he has wronged his soul, so forgive. The man given wisdom and knowledge uses that gift first as conscience, not as self-justification.",
  "16": "\"He pleaded, 'My Lord! I have definitely wronged my soul, so forgive me.'\" Moses' first long speech in this chapter is a prayer, not a sermon to Pharaoh. \"Zalamtu nafsi,\" I have wronged my soul, is the same confession the queen of Sheba made in the last chapter. Two different stories, one honest sentence. Harming another, even in a surge of help, is first a wrong against one's own soul before Allah.\n\n\"So forgive me\" asks \"maghfirah,\" covering and removal of the sin. He does not bargain with his good upbringing or his destiny as a messenger. He asks.\n\n\"So He forgave him, for He is indeed the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\" The answer is immediate in the narration. The names \"al-Ghafur\" and \"al-Rahim\" match the plea. Forgiveness is not because the dead man mattered little. It is because Moses turned at once, and because Allah's mercy meets the one who admits.\n\nThis scene protects two truths. Prophets are protected from Allah in the ways He wills, and this prophet is shown seeking pardon for a fatal blow. The Quran does not hide the blow. It also does not leave Moses in it. Forgiveness becomes part of his preparation. A man who knows he needed mercy can later carry a message of mercy and warning to Pharaoh.\n\nThe previous chapter said fear is only for those who do wrong, but if they mend evil with good, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. Moses lives that verse. He will still fear arrest in the next passage, and he will still be sent as a messenger. Forgiveness is not the end of struggle. It is the cleaning of a stain so the struggle can continue toward the fire, the staff, and the sea.",
  "17": "\"Moses pledged, 'My Lord! For all Your favours upon me, I will never side with the wicked.'\" Forgiveness in the previous verse is answered with a vow. He does not treat pardon as a chance to return to the same heat. He ties the favours, \"ni'am,\" already on him—rescue as a baby, return to his mother, wisdom, knowledge, and now forgiveness—to a new loyalty: he will not be a backer of \"al-mujrimin,\" the wicked.\n\n\"I will never side with\" is a refusal to be a helper, a stay, a support. The punch happened when he sided, in a rush, with one of his people against a foe. The vow is not a vow to abandon the oppressed. It is a vow not to become a partner of crime. Help must not mean joining wickedness.\n\nFavours create debt in a healthy soul. Solomon asked to be inspired to thanks. Moses converts thanks into a moral alliance. The wicked in Egypt include Pharaoh's system, but the vow is broader than one regime. Anyone who is mujrim is not his party.\n\nA listener might think the vow means Moses will never again help an Israelite. The coming verses show an Israelite crying to him again, and Moses calling that man a trouble-maker. The vow is already working as caution. He has learned that a wrong helper can pull a prophet-to-be into blood.\n\nThe sentence is spoken to \"My Lord,\" not to a political club. The audience of the pledge is Allah. That makes it a worship, not a slogan. The next morning of the story will test the pledge in the same city, with fear, watching, and another fight.",
  "18": "\"And so Moses became fearful, watching out in the city.\" Forgiveness did not erase consequences. A man is dead. The city is Pharaoh's. Moses moves in fear, \"kha'if,\" and watchfulness, looking about. The gifted wisdom now includes the wisdom of caution.\n\n\"When suddenly the one who sought his help the day before cried out to him again for help.\" The same Israelite is in another quarrel. He has learned that Moses can strike. He uses him as a weapon a second time. The oppressed man is not simply a pure symbol. He can also be reckless.\n\n\"Moses rebuked him, 'Indeed, you are clearly a trouble-maker.'\" The vow shows its fruit. Yesterday's helper is today's \"ghawi,\" a man of misguidance and trouble. Moses sees a pattern: this person drags others into conflict. The rebuke is public enough to be heard, and it will be used against Moses in the next verse.\n\nFear and moral clarity sit together. He is afraid of arrest, yet he will not blindly punch again. The city that was entered unnoticed is now a place of surveillance, his own and others'.\n\nThe verse is honest about intra-community harm. Not every cry for help is a just cry. A people can be oppressed as a group and still contain a trouble-maker. Moses' rebuke protects the pledge not to side with the wicked, even when the wickedness is messy street fighting that could end in another corpse. The enemy's mouth, however, will twist the almost-raised hand into a charge of tyranny.",
  "19": "\"Then when Moses was about to lay his hands on their foe, the enemy said, 'O Moses! Do you intend to kill me as you killed a man yesterday?'\" The scene speeds up. Moses moves to intervene again, perhaps to stop the foe, perhaps in anger. The Egyptian, or the opponent, knows yesterday's secret. The killing is no longer hidden. It becomes a sentence in the street.\n\n\"You only want to be a tyrant in the land. You do not intend to make peace!\" The charge steals Pharaoh's own title of corruption and pins it on Moses. \"Jabbar,\" a tyrant, a compulsive force. \"Islah,\" making peace or setting things right, is denied to him. One punch is rewritten as a career of oppression.\n\nThe irony is loud for anyone who heard the opening: Pharaoh slaughtered sons, and a man who struck once in a fight is called the tyrant. False accusation is a tool of the powerful and of the desperate alike. Here it may also be a sincere fear from a man who saw death in Moses' hand.\n\nThe public naming of yesterday's killing is the turning point. Moses can no longer live as an unnoticed man in the city. The next verse will send a runner from the farthest end of the city with news of a death plot by the chiefs.\n\nFor the believer, the verse warns how quickly a mistake becomes a brand. Moses has already asked forgiveness. The land has not forgiven. Allah's pardon and people's charge can exist on the same day. The story will not stay to litigate the street. It will move him toward Midian, where a different kind of strength and trustworthiness will be noticed by a woman at a well.",
  "20": "\"And there came a man, rushing from the farthest end of the city.\" Rescue takes the form of a runner. He comes from the far side, \"aqsa al-madinah,\" so the news is city-wide, not a rumor in one alley. He is in a hurry. Time is short.\n\n\"He said, 'O Moses! The chiefs are actually conspiring against you to put you to death, so leave the city.'\" The \"mala',\" the chiefs, the same kind of elite circle that appeared around the queen and around Thamud, are now in council for execution. The punch has been raised to a capital case. The advice is simple: leave.\n\n\"I really advise you to do so.\" He stresses that the counsel is sincere. In a city of unawareness and plots, a sincere adviser is a mercy. Many reports call him a believer from Pharaoh's family who hid his faith, a figure mentioned in another chapter as a man concealing belief. This verse does not name him. It records the act: he risks the run and the speech.\n\nMoses had pledged not to side with the wicked. The chiefs are siding against him with a killing plan, like Thamud's nine. Allah's plan, once again, includes a human warning before the blow.\n\n\"Leave\" becomes the next obedience. The baby once left his house for the river. The man now leaves the city for the desert. Both exits are under threat of death. Both are roads toward messengerhood. The next verse will show him departing in fear and caution, with a prayer to be delivered from the wrongdoing people.",
  "21": "\"So Moses left the city in a state of fear and caution.\" He obeys the unnamed adviser. The leaving is not swagger. It is \"kha'ifan yatarraqab,\" afraid and watchful, the same pair that marked him in the streets. Prophetic destiny does not cancel the human look over the shoulder.\n\n\"Praying, 'My Lord! Deliver me from the wrongdoing people.'\" The road begins with a du'a. \"Najjini,\" deliver me, the same family of words used for the rescue of Salih's faithful and Lot's family. The \"wrongdoing people\" are Pharaoh's folk, the zalimun, the ones who slaughter sons and now hunt him.\n\nHe does not pray to be made king of the city he flees. He prays to be extracted. Delivery is enough. The man who yesterday pledged not to side with the wicked now asks to be separated from them physically.\n\nFear plus prayer is a pattern in this chapter. The mother was told not to fear and was promised return. Moses fears and asks for delivery. Both are believers. The feelings are handled by turning to Allah, not by pretending courage as unbelief in danger.\n\nThe city that picked him from the river as a baby now expels him as a man. The grief he was to become for Pharaoh has begun, but not in the form of a palace revolt. It begins as a fugitive's prayer. The next verse will name the direction: Midian, and a trust that his Lord will guide him to the right way.",
  "22": "\"And as he made his way towards Midian, he said, 'I trust my Lord will guide me to the right way.'\" Midian lies beyond Egypt's grip, a land associated with the people of the prophet Shu'ayb in other passages, though this verse does not pause to lecture geography. What matters is the sentence on the road.\n\n\"I trust,\" \"asa,\" I hope with expectation, that \"my Lord will guide me to the right way.\" He does not claim he already knows every wadi. He claims the Lord as guide. The previous chapter asked who guides in the darkness of the land and sea. Moses answers that question with his feet and his hope.\n\nThe right way is both a physical track across Sinai's approaches and a moral road after blood, fear, and exile. Guidance for a lost fugitive is water, shade, work, and a household. Guidance for a future messenger is the long preparation of eight or ten years that an old man will later offer.\n\nThis trust is tawakkul in motion. He walks. He does not sit in Egypt waiting for the chiefs to become kind. He also does not credit his own map as sufficient. The combination will appear again when he waters a flock and then asks Allah for any provision stored for him.\n\nListeners who flee harm, whether from a city of persecution like early Mecca or from a sin they regret, are given a line to carry: the Lord can still guide to the right way after a messy yesterday. The well of Midian is about to prove that the trust was well placed.",
  "23": "\"When he arrived at the well of Midian, he found a group of people watering their herds.\" The right way first becomes a well. Men are there, a crowd taking water. Moses arrives as a stranger with no flock of his own.\n\n\"Apart from them, he noticed two women holding back their herd.\" The detail is careful. The women are not mixing in the crush. They hold their animals back, waiting. Moses sees what others ignore: a restraint that means need.\n\n\"He asked them, 'What is the problem?'\" A fugitive who is tired and afraid still asks. The question is service, not romance and not a sermon. \"Ma khatbukum,\" what is your affair?\n\n\"They replied, 'We cannot water our animals until the other shepherds are done, for our father is a very old man.'\" Two reasons: custom or force keeps them last, and their father cannot take the men's place because of age. They explain themselves with dignity. They are not begging with shame. They state a fact of weakness in the household.\n\nThe scene quietly condemns the shepherds who will not make room for the daughters of an old man. It also prepares Moses' character test. Strength used here will not be a punch that kills. It will be lifting water for those who wait.\n\nThe previous chapter honored a queen's consultation. This chapter honors two women at a well who speak plainly. The clear Book's truth includes their sentence about an old father. The next verse is Moses' response: he waters, then goes to shade, then prays his need.",
  "24": "\"So he watered their herd for them, then withdrew to the shade.\" He solves the problem without a price talk. Then he leaves. He does not linger to be praised by the daughters. Shade is the rest of a tired traveler, like the night made for rest in the previous chapter.\n\n\"And prayed, 'My Lord! I am truly in desperate need of whatever provision You may have in store for me.'\" After giving, he asks. The order is beautiful. He has spent strength he barely has. Now he admits \"faqir,\" poverty of need, toward any \"khayr,\" any good, Allah has sent down for him.\n\nThe prayer matches the verse about the distressed who cry and are answered. Moses is mudtarr: homeless, hunted, hungry, unmarried, unemployed. He does not specify bread or a job or a wife. He opens the request to whatever good is stored.\n\nMany commentators have noted that the answer came as a meal, a marriage, and years of safety, all through the invitation that follows. The verse itself stops at the need. It teaches the wording of a person who has nothing and still has a Lord.\n\nWithdrawing to the shade also shows haya', a modest holding back. He is not a man who waters and then attaches himself. The next verse will send one of the two women walking bashfully with her father's invitation. His prayer and her modesty will meet in a house where he can tell his whole story and hear, have no fear.",
  "25": "\"Then one of the two women came to him, walking bashfully.\" The English \"bashfully\" renders \"haya',\" shy modesty. The Quran praises the manner of her walk. Invitation after a service can be done with boldness that harms, or with a reserve that keeps dignity for both. She chooses the second.\n\n\"She said, 'My father is inviting you so he may reward you for watering our animals for us.'\" The invitation is from the father. The reason is a reward for work. The frame is halal and clear. No one needs to invent a tale around the well.\n\n\"When Moses came to him and told him his whole story, the old man said, 'Have no fear! You are now safe from the wrongdoing people.'\" Moses does not hide the killing, the fear, or the flight. The \"whole story\" is truth-telling. The old man's first gift is not food. It is a sentence that answers Moses' road prayer: no fear, safety, the zalimun cannot reach you here.\n\nSafety is the provision stored for him, or the beginning of it. The man who left Egypt asking to be delivered hears delivery in a stranger's living room.\n\nReports often identify the old man as Shu'ayb, though some scholars hesitated because of timeline questions. The Quran does not name him here. It names his justice and hospitality. That is enough for the lesson. A home can be a shelter Allah builds for a fugitive who still tells the truth about his worst day. The next verse will add the daughter's judgment of Moses as strong and trustworthy, the two qualities of a good hire.",
  "26": "\"One of the two daughters suggested, 'O my dear father! Hire him.'\" Counsel comes from a daughter, as counsel came from the queen's chiefs and from Solomon's court. This counsel is brief and practical: hire. She has seen him work. She has seen his manner.\n\n\"The best man for employment is definitely the strong and trustworthy one.\" Two conditions, \"qawi\" and \"amin.\" Strength without trust is a danger, like an ifrit without honesty, or like a punch without control. Trust without strength cannot water a flock against a crowd of shepherds. Together they make the best hire.\n\nShe speaks in a proverb so it does not sound like mere personal liking. The rule is general. Moses happens to fit it. Her father can test the rule.\n\nThe verse honors a woman's professional judgment in a family matter. The clear Book is not embarrassed to let a daughter define labor ethics. In a chapter about Pharaoh's corruption of labor and life, this small sentence is a repair: work should go to the strong and the trustworthy.\n\nMoses fled a charge of wanting to be a tyrant. A witness from another land now certifies the opposite: strength yoked to trust. The next verse will turn hire into a marriage proposal with a term of eight or ten years, an old man's wish that is careful not to make the contract difficult.",
  "27": "\"The old man proposed, 'I wish to marry one of these two daughters of mine to you, provided that you stay in my service for eight years.'\" The reward and the hire become a nikah offer. One of the two daughters—the verse does not force us to name which—will be a wife if Moses serves eight years. The dower is labor, a known practice the Quran records without scorn.\n\n\"If you complete ten, it will be a favour from you, but I do not wish to make it difficult for you.\" The contract has a minimum and a voluntary extra. The old man states that he does not want \"shaqq,\" hardship, for Moses. Generosity is written into the terms.\n\n\"Allah willing, you will find me an agreeable man.\" He does not claim saintly perfection. He hopes, with \"in sha' Allah,\" that Moses will find him \"min al-salihin\" in the sense of good to deal with, agreeable. The future in-law puts his own character under a wish and under Allah's will.\n\nThe proposal is the stored provision taking a stable form: family, work, years of distance from Pharaoh. Eight or ten years will mature the man who already had strength and knowledge. A messenger's life can include a long quiet job.\n\nThe fairness of the old man contrasts with Pharaoh's labor of slaughter. One house uses men as victims. This house uses a contract both sides can honor. The next verse is Moses accepting, setting the terms as settled, and calling Allah as Witness.",
  "28": "\"Moses responded, 'Then it is settled between you and I.'\" He accepts without haggling the daughters or the years. \"Dhalika bayni wa baynak\" makes the agreement mutual and clear. A hunted man might grab any offer in panic. Moses still speaks as a contracting party with rights.\n\n\"Whichever term I fulfill, there will be no further obligation on me.\" He locks the old man's own generosity. Eight is enough. Ten is extra. When either term is done, no one may say he still owes. Clarity now prevents dispute later. That is part of being trustworthy.\n\n\"And Allah is a Witness to what we say.\" The Witness, \"wakil\" in the sense of overseeing witness, lifts a family contract into worship. They do not need a Pharaoh's court to notarize. Allah heard the well prayer and now hears the marriage-and-labor terms.\n\nThe verse teaches that even a future prophet binds himself with clean speech. It also teaches that extra work, if given, is a favour, not a chain. Many believers have used this story as a model for fair employment and for marriage without pompous display.\n\nThe next scene will jump: the term is completed, he travels with his family, and he spots a fire on the side of Mount Tur. The years the contract covered are left in silence, which is itself a kind of honor. What mattered was the fulfillment. What comes next is the call that the mother was promised when she put him in the river: We will make him one of the messengers.",
  "29": "\"When Moses had completed the term and was travelling with his family, he spotted a fire on the side of Mount Tur.\" The contract's end is stated first. He kept his word. Now he is a man with a family on a journey, not a lone fugitive. Mount Tur, the mountain of the coming call, stands in the path.\n\n\"He said to his family, 'Stay here, for I have spotted a fire. Perhaps from there I can bring you some directions or a torch from the fire so you may warm yourselves.'\" The wording is almost the same as the previous chapter's opening of Moses' story. A fire, directions, a torch, warmth. The clear Book repeats the human reason so the divine interruption will be felt.\n\nHe is still the helper. At the well he watered. Here he goes toward heat and a path for his family. The man who prayed for any stored good now has people to keep warm. Provision has grown into responsibility.\n\nThe two chapters' tellings support each other. There, the fire led to \"Blessed is the one at the fire\" and \"I am Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise.\" Here, more geography will be given: the bush, the sacred ground, the right side of the valley. Part of the story, as this surah promised, is being filled in.\n\nThe family is told to stay. The call will be for him first. The next verse is the voice from the bush: O Moses! It is truly I. I am Allah—the Lord of all worlds.",
  "30": "\"But when he came to it, he was called from the bush in the sacred ground to the right side of the valley.\" The fire is not only heat. A voice comes from the tree, \"al-shajarah,\" in a blessed plot of ground, \"al-buq'ah al-mubarakah,\" on the valley's right side. Place is honored by the Speaker, not by an idol in the bush.\n\n\"O Moses! It is truly I. I am Allah—the Lord of all worlds.\" The previous chapter had, \"It is truly I. I am Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise.\" This chapter names \"the Lord of all worlds,\" the title the queen used in her last confession. The same Speaker, more of His names.\n\n\"It is truly I\" removes confusion. This is not a jinn of the desert, not a trick of cold and tired eyes. The Lord of all worlds addresses the shepherd by name, the name his mother gave, the name Pharaoh's house used, the name the old man hired.\n\nSacred ground teaches that some earth is made blessed by Allah's choice, as Mecca was made sacred in the last chapter's close. The valley's right side gives the event a map. Revelation enters history at a real location.\n\nThe infant of the river, the killer of a day, the hire of eight or ten years, now stands in a conversation that will send him back to the city he fled. The promise to his mother is being completed. The next command will be the staff, the running, and the reassurance: draw near, have no fear, you are perfectly secure.",
  "31": "\"Now, throw down your staff!\" The first task of messengerhood is an action with a familiar object. The staff of a shepherd becomes a sign. The command is the same as in the previous chapter.\n\n\"But when he saw it slithering like a snake, he ran away without looking back.\" The human body tells the truth. A staff that moves like a \"jann,\" a swift snake, is terrifying. Moses runs and does not return on his tracks. The Quran is not ashamed of that run. It records it twice in two chapters so that fear is not edited out of a prophet's night.\n\n\"Allah reassured him, 'O Moses! Draw near, and have no fear. You are perfectly secure.'\" The last chapter said messengers should have no fear in My presence, and that fear is for those who do wrong. This chapter adds \"draw near\" and \"you are perfectly secure,\" \"min al-aminin.\" Security after a run is a gift. He is not scolded as a coward. He is invited closer.\n\nThe man who left Egypt in fear, who was told by an old man to have no fear, now hears the Lord of all worlds say have no fear. Each stage answers the same wound with a higher speaker.\n\n\"Draw near\" is the opposite of the chiefs' \"leave the city.\" The world pushed him out. Allah pulls him in. Nearness is the beginning of the mission that will require him to walk toward Pharaoh, the source of the old fear. The next verse will add the white hand, the calming of the arms, and the naming of the two proofs for Pharaoh and his chiefs.",
  "32": "\"Now put your hand through the opening of your collar, it will come out shining white, unblemished.\" The second sign is the hand, \"bayda' min ghayri su',\" white without disease. It is not leprosy. It is a light of proof. The same sign was listed among nine signs in the previous chapter.\n\n\"And cross your arms tightly to calm your fears.\" A practical mercy sits between miracles. He is told a gesture to settle the fright that made him run. Revelation includes care for the nerves. The messenger is not asked to pretend he has no body.\n\n\"These are two proofs from your Lord to Pharaoh and his chiefs. They have truly been a rebellious people.\" The staff and the hand are \"burhanan,\" two clear proofs, from \"your Lord,\" not from Moses' skill. The audience is Pharaoh and the mala', the chiefs who once plotted Moses' death. The verdict on them is already spoken: they have been \"fasiqin,\" rebellious, breaking bounds.\n\nSending a fugitive back to the court that wanted him killed, armed with a stick and a shining hand, is the will to show Pharaoh what he feared. The proofs are for them, whether they accept or call it magic.\n\nThe next two verses will let Moses speak his remaining human fear: he has killed one of them, and his tongue is not as eloquent as Aaron's. The Lord who told him he is perfectly secure will answer those fears with assistance, authority, and a promise that he and his followers will prevail with the signs.",
  "33": "\"Moses appealed, 'My Lord! I have indeed killed a man from them, so I fear they may kill me.'\" Security in the valley does not erase memory of the street. He reminds his Lord of the death. The fear is specific: qisas, revenge, execution. A prophet may present his fear as a request for help, not as a refusal.\n\nThe verse shows that \"have no fear\" is a process. He is secure in Allah's presence. He still thinks about Pharaoh's prison. That thinking is brought to the same Lord in an \"appeal.\" Prayer continues to be his method, from the shade of Midian to the sacred ground.\n\nHe does not deny the killing or rename it. \"I have indeed killed a man from them\" is still confession. Forgiveness was given years before. Consequences in the political world remain. He asks, in effect, for a mission plan that takes those consequences into account.\n\nThe next verse will add a second need: Aaron's eloquence. Together the two verses are not cowardice. They are a messenger doing istisharah with his Lord, laying out obstacles. The chiefs of Sheba offered military might. Moses offers honesty about blood and about speech. Allah will not reject the appeal. He will pair him with his brother and grant authority so they cannot harm him.\n\nListeners who are asked to face people they have wronged, or people who believe they have been wronged, can hear that even Moses named the obstacle instead of pretending a clean record in the enemy's file.",
  "34": "\"And my brother Aaron is more eloquent than I, so send him with me as a helper to support what I say.\" The second appeal is about speech. \"Afsah\" more eloquent. Moses feels a tightness of tongue, mentioned in other verses as a knot he asked to be loosened. He does not ask to stay home. He asks for a helper, \"rid',\" a support who will confirm his words.\n\n\"For I truly fear they may reject me.\" The fear now is not only killing. It is rejection, \"yukadhdhibun,\" they will call him a liar. Pharaoh's court is a place of talkers and magicians. Moses wants a brother beside the two proofs.\n\nFamily returns as mercy. A mother, a sister, a wife, and now a brother. The oppressed house of Israel is being rebuilt as a team of revelation. Aaron is not a rival prophet in Moses' mouth. He is a helper for the message.\n\nAsking for a partner is allowed. The Prophet Muhammad was told in other places that Allah is enough, yet Moses is granted this request openly. Different missions, same Lord who knows what each messenger needs.\n\nThe next verse is the response: We will assist you with your brother and grant you both authority, so they cannot harm you. The two fears, death and rejection, are met with assistance, authority, and a promise of prevailing with signs. Moses' appeal is honored as part of the truth of the story, not edited into a man who never asked for help.",
  "35": "\"Allah responded, 'We will assist you with your brother and grant you both authority, so they cannot harm you.'\" The answer is \"sanashuddu 'adudaka,\" We will strengthen your arm with your brother. Authority, \"sultan,\" is a warrant they cannot brush aside as a private quarrel about a dead man. Harm is denied: they will not reach them with the killing they intend.\n\n\"With Our signs, you and those who follow you will certainly prevail.\" Victory is tied to \"ayatina,\" Our signs, not to a new Egyptian army. Followers are included. The oppressed who were promised succession in the opening verses appear here as those who follow the two brothers. Prevail, \"al-ghalibun,\" the overcoming ones.\n\nThe promise does not say Pharaoh will believe. It says he cannot harm them in the way that stops the mission, and that the followers will end as winners. The sea, the drowning, and Israel's leaving are the later fulfillment. This verse states the warrant before the confrontation.\n\nMoses asked about a past killing and a weak tongue. Allah answers with a future of authority and dominance through signs. The scale jumps. Human obstacles are real. They are not the ceiling.\n\nFor believers in Mecca, hearing that a hunted man would prevail with his brother and his followers was not a history lesson only. It was a mirror. A community that recites and follows can be told it will prevail without becoming Pharaoh. The next verse will show how Pharaoh's court meets the clear signs: they will call them conjured magic and claim they never heard of this from their forefathers.",
  "36": "\"But when Moses came to them with Our clear signs, they said arrogantly, 'This is nothing but conjured magic tricks.'\" The mission begins. The signs are \"bayyinat,\" clear. The response is a label: \"sihrun muftara,\" magic invented, conjured, made up. The previous chapter recorded the same habit: when enlightening signs came, they said this is pure magic. Two tellings, one disease.\n\n\"We have never heard of this in the history of our forefathers.\" The second shield is ancestry. If the fathers did not know this worship and this kind of sign, it must be false. The last chapter's pagans called resurrection ancient fables. Pharaoh's chiefs call monotheism a novelty their fathers never heard. Both insults abuse history, one by calling truth too old, one by calling it too new.\n\nArrogance is named in the English because the tone is high. Clear signs should lower a person. They raise a defense instead. Hearts can be convinced, as the other chapter said, and tongues still say magic.\n\nThe verse is a warning to anyone who judges revelation by whether it matches the family file. Forefathers can be wrong. Pharaoh's fathers built the very corruption this Book began by condemning.\n\nMoses has arrived with what he was given in the valley. The court's first sentence is already rejection, the thing he feared. Allah's promise of prevailing will now have to walk through this insult. The next verses will continue the debate, including Pharaoh's claim of no other god and his order to Haman to build a tower. The pattern of the clear Book holds: truth is narrated, and arrogance is quoted so it can be known.",
  "37": "\"Moses responded, 'My Lord knows best who has come with true guidance from Him'\" answers the court's claim that his signs are invented magic and unknown to the fathers. He does not enter a shouting match about ancestry. He refers the file to Allah. The Lord knows who actually brought \"huda,\" guidance, from Him.\n\n\"And will fare best in the end.\" The measure is \"al-aqibah,\" the final outcome, not the palace's present noise. Guidance is proved by how a matter ends, the same \"see then the end\" that followed Pharaoh in the last chapter and Thamud's plan.\n\n\"Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed.\" \"Al-zalimun\" will not have \"falah,\" lasting success. They may have bricks, towers, and armies. They do not have the end. The sentence is a principle, not a wish.\n\nMoses, who once feared their killing, now speaks like a man under the authority he was promised. The pledge not to side with the wicked has become a public theology: wrongdoers do not succeed.\n\nThe next verse will show Pharaoh trying to succeed by a tower to look at the God of Moses. That project is the opposite of Moses' referral to the Lord's knowledge. One man points up with clay. One man points to the Knower. The end will cast the clay-builder into the sea.",
  "38": "\"Pharaoh declared, 'O chiefs! I know of no other god for you but myself.'\" The debate is dragged down to a political creed. He does not say he made the heavens. He says he knows no god for them except himself. The chiefs are given a god who can kill them. \"I know\" is a tyrant's knowledge: what he permits to be known.\n\n\"So bake bricks out of clay for me, O Haman, and build a high tower so I may look at the God of Moses, although I am sure he is a liar.\" Haman, named at the chapter's start among the sinful, receives an engineering order. Clay, fire, a tower, a pretended observation of heaven. The mockery is that Moses' Lord might be spotted from a scaffold. The aside, \"I am sure he is a liar,\" tells the chiefs the research is already finished.\n\nThe tower is propaganda. It treats the unseen Lord as a bird one might see from a roof. It also keeps labor busy in a project of pride. The oppressed who made bricks in other tellings of this story hear their labor used to insult their God.\n\nThe previous chapter asked who created the heavens. Pharaoh answers, in effect, I am your god, and I will peek at Moses' God with baked clay. The mismatch is the teaching. The next verse states the root: he and his soldiers behaved arrogantly in the land with no right, thinking they would never be returned.",
  "39": "\"And so he and his soldiers behaved arrogantly in the land with no right.\" The tower talk is part of a larger \"istikbar,\" arrogance, shared by the army. \"Bighayr al-haqq,\" with no right. Height without right is the definition of Pharaoh's elevation in verse four. Soldiers are included so that no one thinks only the king's tongue was guilty.\n\n\"Thinking they would never be returned to Us.\" The engine of the arrogance is denial of return, \"la yurja'un.\" If there is no coming back to Allah, a man can be a god in a valley of clay. If there is return, the tower is a joke against himself.\n\nThe last chapter spent many verses on people who called resurrection a fable and asked when. Pharaoh lives that denial as policy. His soldiers live it as obedience. Thought, \"zann,\" a reckoning that they would not be returned, is enough to unlock slaughter and mockery.\n\nThe Quran often ties pride on earth to denial of the meeting with Allah. This verse is a clean statement of that tie. The next verse is the return they did not expect, not yet the Last Day but a sea that acts like a court: We seized him and his soldiers, casting them into the sea. See then what was the end of the wrongdoers.",
  "40": "\"So We seized him and his soldiers, casting them into the sea.\" The seizure, \"akhadhnahu,\" is Allah's taking. The soldiers who shared arrogance share the throw. The sea, which they thought they could cross to finish Israel, becomes a grave. Other passages describe the parting and the closing. This verse states the moral fact: cast into the sea.\n\n\"See then what was the end of the wrongdoers!\" The refrain returns. Eyes are commanded again. The end of zalimun is not a theory. It is a shoreline. Moses had said the wrongdoers will never succeed. This is that sentence in salt water.\n\nThe infant picked from a river lives. The tyrant is thrown into a sea. Water bookends the story. Allah's will to favor the oppressed has reached a visible stage.\n\n\"See then\" also speaks to Quraysh, who had no Nile but had pride and a denial of return. Ends can be seen in stories if people will look. The next two verses will extend Pharaoh's end beyond the drowning: leaders to the Fire, no help on Judgment Day, a curse in this world, and outcast status in the next. The sea was not the whole payment.",
  "41": "\"We made them leaders inviting others to the Fire.\" After death, Pharaoh and his circle remain \"a'immah,\" leaders, but their imamate is toward the Fire. They invited to themselves as gods and chiefs. That invitation is unveiled as an invitation to Hell. A leader is known by where he takes people.\n\n\"And on the Day of Judgment they will not be helped.\" No soldier, no Haman, no tower, no ancestry will be a helper, \"yunsarun.\" The chiefs who conspired to kill Moses cannot conspire against the Fire.\n\nThe contrast with verse five is sharp. Allah willed to make the oppressed models and successors. He made these men models of ruin. Both are \"making\" of Allah in history, one as favor, one as just consequence of their own invitation.\n\nPeople sometimes excuse followers of a tyrant as having no choice. This verse treats the soldiers as leaders in their own rank, inviting others. Obedience in crime is a form of leadership toward the Fire.\n\nThe next verse adds a curse that follows them in this world and outcast status on the Day. Their brand does not wash off with the sea. History remembers them as a warning, which is already a curse on the tongue of later nations.",
  "42": "\"We caused a curse to follow them in this world.\" The drowning is followed by \"la'nah,\" a curse, chasing them in worldly memory and in the wrath of Allah that stays attached to their names. To be cursed in this world is to become a byword of evil, a story told against oneself.\n\n\"And on the Day of Judgment they will be among the outcasts.\" \"Al-maqbuhin\" are those made ugly, rejected, driven away from mercy. The two worlds agree against them. There is no rehabilitation of Pharaoh's creed at the court of the next life.\n\nThe chapter opened with a will to show them what they feared. It now shows the listener what they became. Fear of Israel's rise was small beside this curse.\n\nA curse following in the world also warns living tyrants that even if they die in bed, the language of later people may still hunt their names. Pharaoh did not die in bed. He still stands as the Quran's most repeated political warning.\n\nThe story of the drowning pauses here so the Book can turn to what came after for Israel: the Scripture given to Moses as insight, guide, and mercy. Judgment on Egypt and revelation for the delivered people are two sides of one Lord.",
  "43": "\"Indeed, We gave Moses the Scripture—after destroying earlier nations—as an insight for the people, a guide, and mercy so perhaps they would be mindful.\" The Torah, the Scripture of Moses, is given after \"ma ahlakna al-qurun al-ula,\" after earlier generations were destroyed. Revelation arrives on a stage already marked by ruins. Insight, \"basa'ir,\" is seeing-power for people. Guide and mercy match descriptions of the Quran in the previous chapter. The purpose is \"la'allahum yatadhakkarun,\" that they may be mindful.\n\nThe oppressed who became successors need a Book. Victory without Scripture would become another Pharaoh. Allah does not leave the saved with only a sea-miracle. He gives insight.\n\n\"After destroying earlier nations\" reminds Israel, and reminds Mecca, that the Book is not the first warning in history. Peoples have already fallen. Mindfulness is the reasonable response.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad is about to be told he was not present at that giving of the Scripture. The point of this verse is to set the gift in time. The next verses will use Moses' unseen moments as proof that the Quran's narrator is Allah, not a man who attended Midian and Tur as a tourist.",
  "44": "\"You were not there, O Prophet, on the western side of the mountain when We entrusted the Commandments to Moses, nor were you present in his time.\" The address jumps to Muhammad. He did not stand on the western side, \"al-janib al-gharbi,\" when the \"amr,\" the Command, was given to Moses. He was not \"of the witnesses\" in that century.\n\nThe argument is simple and strong. The story's details—western side, command entrusted—are not Meccan campfire knowledge in this form. The man reciting them was not a witness. Therefore the recitation is from the One who was there, the \"We\" who entrusted.\n\nThis protects the Prophet from the charge of copying travelers' tales as if he had researched a mountain. It also honors him: he receives what only a revealer can give.\n\nThe previous chapter ended by commanding him to recite the Quran. This verse explains how recitation can include a mountain he never saw. The next verse will add that he did not live among Midian's people rehearsing revelations. Two absences, one source.",
  "45": "\"But We later raised several generations, and the ages took their toll on them.\" Between Moses and Muhammad many generations were \"ansha'na,\" raised up, and \"tatawala 'alayhim al-'umur,\" long ages stretched over them. Memory fades. Scripture is altered or neglected. The long time explains why a new recitation is needed and why details of Midian and Tur are not common property.\n\n\"Nor were you living among the people of Midian, rehearsing Our revelations with them.\" A second alibi against human learning: he did not dwell in Midian reciting verses to that town. The well, the two women, the contract of years, are not his travel diary.\n\n\"But it is We Who have sent this revelation to you.\" The close names the sender. Absence from the mountain and from Midian plus presence of the story equals sending from Allah.\n\nThe verse comforts a Prophet accused of being taught by a foreign person. Long ages and two geographies he never inhabited stand between him and the events. The sender bridges the gap. The next verse will add a third absence: he was not at Tur when Allah called, and will state the purpose of his sending: a mercy, a warning to a people who had no recent warner.",
  "46": "\"And you were not at the side of Mount Tur when We called out to Moses.\" A third \"you were not.\" The call from the bush, already narrated, is claimed as a moment the Prophet did not attend. The One who called is the One who now tells.\n\n\"But you have been sent as a mercy from your Lord to warn a people to whom no warner has come before you, so perhaps they may be mindful.\" The sending has a positive reason, not only a proof of source. He is a mercy, \"rahmah.\" His work is to warn. The people are those without a recent messenger, the Arabs of the Mother City and its surroundings in the long gap after earlier prophets. The hope is mindfulness, the same hope attached to Moses' Scripture.\n\n\"No warner before you\" means no warner to this people in their immediate history, not that the world had no prophets. Ishmael's line had gone long without a messenger among them. The Quran elsewhere says the Prophet is sent to a people whose fathers were not warned.\n\nMercy and warning belong together, as guide and mercy belonged together in the last chapter. A warning that no one brought for ages is itself a kindness. The next verse adds another reason for sending: so they cannot say, if struck, that they would have believed if only a messenger had come.",
  "47": "\"Also so they would not say, if struck by an affliction for what their hands have done: 'Our Lord! If only You had sent us a messenger, we would have followed Your revelations and become believers.'\" A second purpose of the sending is to close an excuse. If a disaster, \"musibah,\" hit them for their own hands' work, they might claim they would have followed ayat and believed if a messenger had only been sent.\n\nAllah sends the messenger first. The excuse is removed before the affliction. This is part of the mercy just mentioned. Justice in the Quran includes not punishing a people until a warner has come, as other verses state.\n\n\"What their hands have done\" keeps blame on deeds, not on fate as an orphan cause. If affliction comes, it answers their work. The hypothetical speech is quoted so the listener can hear how people bargain after the fact.\n\nMecca later did meet afflictions and still argued. The verse shows that the argument was anticipated and answered by the very presence of the Prophet. The next verse will show what they said instead when the truth actually arrived: they demanded the like of what Moses was given, while already denying Moses too.",
  "48": "\"But when the truth came to them from Us, they said, 'If only he was given the like of what Moses had been given.'\" The excuse changes. Now a messenger has come, and they want Moses' package: a staff, a shining hand, a sea, a whole revealed tablet in one throw, as they imagine it. Comparison becomes a stall.\n\n\"Did they not deny what had been given to Moses earlier?\" The Quran turns their comparison against them. Some of them, or their allies among deniers of Moses, already rejected the Torah's signs. Pagans who suddenly honor Moses as a standard are not sincere students of Moses.\n\n\"They claimed, 'Both Scriptures are works of magic, supporting each other!' Adding, 'We truly deny both.'\" When Torah and Quran are seen together, the insult becomes \"sihran,\" two magics that help each other, and a denial of both. The people who asked for the like of Moses reveal that Moses would not have been accepted either.\n\nThe verse unmasks a method: raise the bar, then deny every bar. Moses' people called his signs magic. Mecca calls both books magic. The disease is inherited.\n\nThe next verse will give the Prophet a challenge: bring a scripture from Allah that guides better than these two, if you are truthful. If they reject both, they must produce a third that is better, or admit they are following desire.",
  "49": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Bring then a scripture from Allah which is a better guide than these two so I may follow it, if your claim is true.'\" \"These two\" are the Torah and the Quran, the two they just called magic. The Prophet is told to offer a fair contest. If both are rejected as guidance, let the rejecters produce a Book from Allah that guides better. He even says he would follow it. That is confidence, not fear.\n\nThe condition \"if your claim is true\" puts their denial on trial. Denial is easy. Production is not. The previous chapter demanded proof from those who set up partners. This verse demands a better kitab.\n\nNo one in Mecca produced such a scripture. The challenge still stands against anyone who dismisses both revelations and offers only taste and custom.\n\n\"I may follow it\" also teaches that the Prophet's loyalty is to the best guidance from Allah, not to tribal pride in \"his\" book as a brand. Because these two are from Allah, no better third exists. The offer is sincere in form and impossible in fact, which is the point.\n\nThe next verse says if they fail to respond, they only follow desires, and none is more astray than the one who follows desire without guidance from Allah.",
  "50": "\"So if they fail to respond to you, then know that they only follow their desires.\" Silence after the challenge is itself an answer. No better book appears. What remains is \"ahwa',\" desires, likes, the pull of the self. Their theology is preference.\n\n\"And who could be more astray than those who follow their desires with no guidance from Allah?\" The question is a scale. Desire plus no guidance is the extreme of \"dall,\" being lost. Desire is not always evil as a feeling. Desire as imam, as leader in place of revelation, is ruin.\n\n\"Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.\" Those who choose this path of zulm, putting desire in the place of the Book, are not then given the guidance they refused. The sentence is a law of dealing, not a denial that a wrongdoer can repent. While they persist as zalimun of this kind, guidance is withheld.\n\nThe Prophet is told to \"know\" this. The knowledge protects him from thinking his recitation failed for lack of a cleverer argument. He gave the challenge. They gave desire. The next verse will say Allah has steadily delivered the Word so people may be mindful. Delivery continues even when desire leads some away.",
  "51": "\"Indeed, We have steadily delivered the Word of Allah to the people so they may be mindful.\" \"Wassalna lahumul-qawl\" means the Word has been connected, sent in sequence, kept coming. Revelation did not arrive as one shout and then silence. Verses follow verses, stories follow stories, this chapter follows the last. The purpose is mindfulness.\n\nThe steady delivery is mercy for people who need repetition and time. It is also evidence against those who said they would believe if only a messenger came. The Word has been linked to them.\n\n\"The people\" here includes Mecca and those around the recitation. The previous chapter said the Quran clarifies and is a guide and mercy. This verse describes the method: connected delivery.\n\nMindfulness, \"tadhakkur,\" is remembering what the self already has reason to know: that desire is not a book, that Pharaoh drowned, that a mother trusted a river. Steady words try to wake that memory.\n\nThe next verses will show that some people of earlier Scripture already believe this incoming Word, greet it as truth from their Lord, and will receive a double reward. Steady delivery finds prepared hearts as well as desire-led ones.",
  "52": "\"As for those faithful to whom We had given the Scripture before this Quran, they do believe in it.\" Not all of the People of the Book deny. Those given Scripture before, who were faithful to it, believe in this recitation. The Quran is not a stranger to a heart that already knows revelation.\n\nThe verse comforts the Prophet and honors those Jews and Christians, and any earlier scriptural believers, who recognized Muhammad's message. It also refutes the claim that the new Book is a magic that true students of Moses must reject. True students believe.\n\n\"Before this\" keeps history in order. Allah has been giving books. The latest does not cancel the fact of the earlier gift. It confirms it, as other verses say.\n\nThe next verse will quote their speech when it is recited: we believe, this is truth from our Lord, we had already submitted before this. Their iman is not a vague mood. It has words.",
  "53": "\"When it is recited to them, they declare, 'We believe in it. This is definitely the truth from our Lord.'\" Recitation is the trigger. They hear, they speak. Belief is tied to the heard Quran. \"Al-haqq min rabbina\" matches the chapter's claim to narrate in truth. They recognize the taste of revelation.\n\n\"We had already submitted even before this.\" \"Muslimin\" again. They were people of surrender before the Arabic recitation arrived. Islam, in this sense, is the religion of those who yield to Allah in every age. The Quran finds them already on that road and they step onto the new portion of it.\n\nThis is the opposite of those who said both scriptures are magic. Same recitation, two hearings.\n\nTheir statement also blocks a pride that says no one knew Allah until this century. They knew enough to submit, and this Book confirms them.\n\nThe next verse will name their reward: double, for perseverance, for answering evil with good, and for giving from what they were provided. Belief that speaks at recitation is expected to become a life of patience and charity.",
  "54": "\"These believers will be given a double reward for their perseverance.\" \"Marratayn,\" twice, a doubled wage. Reports and sister verses, such as those about People of the Book who believe in their prophet and in Muhammad, explain a double portion: faith in the first book and faith in the second, or patience in two tests. The verse itself ties the double reward to \"sabr,\" perseverance.\n\n\"Responding to evil with good\" is a second trait. They do not pay slander with slander. \"Hasanah\" meets \"sayyi'ah.\" The next verse will show them turning from idle talk with peace.\n\n\"And for donating from what We have provided for them\" is the third trait. Charity from \"razaqnahum\" keeps the doubled reward from being only a feeling of faith. They spend.\n\nThe three—patience, good in return for evil, spending—are a portrait of the believers this chapter wants, whether from earlier Scripture or from the Arabs. The queen answered a hard letter with thought. These answer a hard environment with good.\n\nDouble reward is bounty like \"whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded with what is better.\" Allah's math is generous to those who carry two books in one heart and still stay gentle. The next verse details how they handle ugly speech.",
  "55": "\"When they hear slanderous talk, they turn away from it.\" \"Laghw\" is vain, ugly, useless speech. They do not wade in it. Turning away is an action of sabr.\n\n\"Saying, 'We are accountable for our deeds and you for yours.'\" They draw a line of responsibility. \"Lana a'maluna wa lakum a'malukum.\" No need to inherit another person's garbage tongue.\n\n\"Peace is our only response to you! We want nothing to do with those who act ignorantly.\" \"Salam\" here is a parting peace, a refusal to fight in the mud, not necessarily the greeting of full alliance. \"We do not want the ignorant,\" \"al-jahilin,\" those acting in jahl, the same word Lot used for people acting ignorantly.\n\nThe verse teaches a method for minorities of faith in a loud city. Not every insult is a debate that must be won in the same tone. Peace and distance can be piety.\n\nThis is also how desire-led talk is treated. The ones who follow hawaa produce laghw. The ones who follow the Word produce salam. The next verse will remind the Prophet that even with such beauty in the message, he cannot guide whom he loves; Allah guides whom He wills.",
  "56": "\"You surely cannot guide whoever you like, O Prophet, but it is Allah Who guides whoever He wills.\" The sentence is famous and heavy. The Prophet's love does not open a heart. Allah's will does. Many reports connect this to his dying uncle Abu Talib, whom he longed to see in iman. The wording, however, is general. Liking, \"man ahbabta,\" whoever you would love to guide, is not the key.\n\n\"And He knows best who are fit to be guided.\" \"A'lamu bil-muhtadin\" He knows the guided, or those ready for guidance. Knowledge and will together replace the Prophet's wish as the decider.\n\nThe previous chapter said he cannot make the dead hear or lead the blind out. This verse says the same with the extra pain of affection. Love is not blamed. It is limited.\n\nA misconception is that the Prophet failed. The verse exists to deny that measure. Another misconception is that people have no responsibility. Other verses in this passage stressed choice, desire, and excuse-making. This verse stresses Allah's prerogative in guidance. Both are to be held.\n\nThe stories of Pharaoh and of the Scripture-believers who said we already submitted sit on either side of this truth. One was loved by no prophet into faith. One believed upon recitation. Allah knew who was guided. The next verses will turn to Meccans who say they follow guidance if they follow you, they would be swept from the land—an excuse of fear that the coming lines will answer with the sanctuary they already enjoy.",
  "57": "\"They say to the Prophet, 'If we were to follow true guidance with you, we would certainly be snatched away from our land.'\" A new excuse, not desire this time but fear. Quraysh claim that joining his guidance would make Arabs snatch them from Mecca. Faith, they say, is geopolitically impossible. Guidance is treated as a threat to real estate.\n\n\"Have We not established for them a safe haven in Mecca to which fruits of all kinds are brought as a provision from Us?\" The answer points at the city they fear to lose. Allah already made it a \"haram,\" a safe sanctuary. Fruits arrive from every side as \"rizq\" from Him. The last chapter called Him the Lord of this city who made it sacred. This verse applies that sacredness to their excuse. The safety they enjoy is already a gift, not an idol's achievement.\n\n\"But most of them do not know this favour.\" They live inside a sanctuary and credit their partners or their own politics. Ignorance of the favour makes the excuse sound wise.\n\nThe argument is: the God who kept Mecca safe while you worshipped others can keep you safe if you worship Him alone. Snatching is not more in the hands of tribes than in His. The next verse will remind them how many comfortable towns were destroyed, so that comfort itself is not a fortress.",
  "58": "\"Imagine how many societies We have destroyed that had been spoiled by their comfortable living!\" \"Batirat ma'ishataha\" means they insolently wasted an easy life. Comfort became arrogance. Destruction came to towns that thought ease was a shield.\n\n\"Those are their residences, never inhabited after them except passingly.\" The houses remain, like Thamud's ruined homes in the last chapter, lived in only a little, by travelers or briefly. Empty luxury is a sermon.\n\n\"And We alone were the Successor.\" \"Nahnu al-warithun,\" We inherited. When people are gone, Allah remains the Owner. Succession they feared losing in Mecca belongs finally to Him.\n\nThe verse answers both greed and fear. Comfortable living did not save others. Being snatched is not the only danger. Being left as vacant rooms is another. The safe haven of Mecca should produce thanks, not the insolence of those ruined societies.\n\nThe next verse states a law of destruction: no society is destroyed until a messenger recites in its center, and not unless its people persist in wrongdoing. Mecca now has the messenger. Persistence is the remaining condition.",
  "59": "\"Your Lord would never destroy a society until He had sent to its capital a messenger, reciting Our revelations to them.\" Justice has a method. The \"umm,\" the mother-town, the capital, receives a messenger who recites. Destruction is not a blind quake on an unwarned people. This matches the earlier purpose of sending so they would not say, if only You had sent a messenger.\n\n\"Nor would We ever destroy a society unless its people persisted in wrongdoing.\" The second condition is their \"zulm\" continuing. Recitation plus persistence equals a people who have chosen. Pharaoh's Egypt had Moses and persisted. Lot's town had Lot and persisted. The law is one.\n\nMecca is the capital now. Recitation is happening. The verse is therefore both a comfort and a warning. Comfort: Allah is not unjust. Warning: the second condition is in their hands.\n\n\"Your Lord\" keeps the Prophet in a relationship of trust. The Lord who would never destroy unfairly is the Lord who told him he cannot guide whom he likes. Both sentences take panic out of the mission. The next verse will scale down the pleasures they fear to lose: they are fleeting enjoyment and adornment beside what is with Allah.",
  "60": "\"Whatever pleasure you have been given is no more than a fleeting enjoyment and adornment of this worldly life.\" The fruits brought to Mecca, the houses, the status, are \"mata'\" and \"zinah,\" use and decoration, of \"al-hayat al-dunya.\" They are real and they are short.\n\n\"But what is with Allah is far better and more lasting.\" Better, \"khayr,\" and more lasting, \"abqa.\" The comparison is quality and time. Desire chose the short. Guidance offers the lasting.\n\n\"Will you not then understand?\" \"Ta'qilun,\" will you use the mind. The excuse about being snatched treated land as the highest good. Understanding would rank the goods.\n\nSolomon had said what Allah granted him was greater than gifts. This verse generalizes that ranking for every listener. The fine promise of the next verse and the worldly enjoyment of the punished will be set as two lives that cannot be equal.\n\nUnderstanding here is not a philosophy class. It is the simple arithmetic of lasting versus fading. People who do not know the favour of the sanctuary also fail this arithmetic. The following question will make the two destinies stand side by side.",
  "61": "\"Can those to whom We have made a fine promise—which they will see fulfilled—be like those who We have allowed to enjoy the pleasures of this worldly life, but on the Day of Judgment will be brought for punishment?\" The question expects no. Two groups cannot be equal. One has a \"wa'd hasan,\" a beautiful promise, and they will meet it. The other is given \"mata'\" of the world, then is hauled to the Day.\n\n\"Allowed to enjoy\" is itself a kind of letting-go, not an honor. Pharaoh was allowed soldiers and a tower-project. Then the sea. Enjoyment without the fine promise is a rope.\n\nThe believers of doubled reward, Moses' mother who knew the promise was true, the oppressed made successors, all sit on the side of the fine promise. The snatched-land worriers sit in danger of the second column.\n\n\"Which they will see fulfilled\" answers delay. The promise is not a fable of the ancients. They will meet it with eyes. The next verses open that Day with a call: where are those you claimed were My associate-gods? The fine promise and the bringing-forth both arrive in speech from the Lord.",
  "62": "\"Watch for the Day He will call to them, 'Where are those you claimed were My associate-gods?'\" The call is a search that everyone knows will fail. \"Shuraka'i,\" My associates, is said from their claim, not from reality. Allah had no partners. They invented a share and attached it to Him.\n\n\"Where\" is the question of the missing. On earth the partners had names, stones, angels wrongly worshipped, ancestors. On that Day the question exposes absence.\n\nThe previous chapter asked which is better, Allah or what they associate. This Day asks where those associates have gone. Argument becomes attendance.\n\nPharaoh said he knew no god for them but himself. He too will be asked in the larger sense of this call, as will Mecca's idol keepers. The next verse will let the misleaders speak: they will admit they led followers into deviance because they were deviant, and they will disassociate.",
  "63": "\"Those misleaders against whom the decree of torment is justified will cry, 'Our Lord! These followers are the ones we caused to deviate.'\" The leaders, \"alladhina haqqa 'alayhimul-qawl,\" the ones the word has come due against, try a confession that is also a shove. We made them deviate.\n\n\"We led them into deviance, for we ourselves were deviant.\" They admit their own \"ghawayah.\" Tempting others was an overflow of their being lost.\n\n\"We disassociate ourselves from them before You. It was not us that they used to worship.\" The last sentence is a cut. Even the leaders deny that they were the true objects of worship. Jinn, idols, and proud humans all shed the followers at the worst time.\n\nThe verse teaches that followership will not be owned by the chiefs on that Day. Pharaoh's soldiers will not find Pharaoh claiming them. Meccan juniors will not find their elders claiming them.\n\nThe next verse will tell the followers to call the associates for help. They will call and hear nothing, and they will wish they had been guided. Both ends of the chain break.",
  "64": "\"It will be said to the disbelievers, 'Call upon your associate-gods for help.'\" The dare they lived by is returned as a command they cannot use. On earth they said these would intercede or bring them nearer. Now, call.\n\n\"So they will call them, but will receive no response.\" The call goes out. \"Lam yastajibu\" they do not answer. The previous chapter asked who responds to the distressed. The associates fail that test in public.\n\n\"And they will face the punishment, wishing they had been rightly guided.\" The wish, \"law annahum kanoo yahtadoon,\" arrives when guidance's door, as they knew it, has closed. Wishing is not the repentance of those who repent, believe, and do good in the next verses. It is regret under the shadow of the seen punishment.\n\nThe psychological end of shirk is a silent sky where the old names used to be. The next call from Allah will not be about partners but about messengers: what response did you give to the messengers?",
  "65": "\"And watch for the Day He will call to them, asking, 'What response did you give to the messengers?'\" A second call, a second file. Not only whom they worshipped, but how they answered the humans sent to them. Moses, the Prophet of Mecca, Salih, Lot, all stand behind this question.\n\n\"Response\" is \"ajabtumu,\" what did you give back. Magic, fables, threats, expulsion, we would be snatched from the land—those are responses. The question makes every slogan of this chapter return as evidence.\n\nMessengers are the mercy that removed the excuse \"if only You had sent.\" So the question is necessary. If a messenger came, the court must ask what was said to him.\n\nThe next verse says they will be too dumbstruck to ask one another for answers. No huddle of chiefs will produce a shared script like Thamud's night oath. The mala' is dissolved.",
  "66": "\"They will be too dumbstruck on that Day to ask one another for answers.\" \"Fa'iyat 'alayhimul-anba'\" the tidings will be obscured to them; they cannot retrieve news or arguments. They do not question each other, \"la yatasa'alun,\" because no one has a useful answer to borrow.\n\nOn earth they lived by consultation of the wrong kind, chiefs telling a queen to use might, Pharaoh telling Haman to bake bricks, nine men swearing together. That social mind fails. Each mouth is empty.\n\nThe previous chapter left some speechless when the decree was justified. This verse shows the same silence as an inability to even confer. Community of denial was a worldly comfort. It is not a next-world resource.\n\nAfter this darkness the next verse opens a window still available now: those who repent, believe, and do good may hope to be among the successful. The dumbstruck Day is not yet. The door is described while speech still works.",
  "67": "\"As for those who repent, believe, and do good in this world, it is right to hope that they will be among the successful.\" Three acts: \"taba,\" turned back; \"amana,\" believed; \"amila salihan,\" did righteous work. The place is this world, while calling still receives a response from the living.\n\n\"It is right to hope,\" \"fa'asa,\" it may well be, a hope that in Allah's speech is near a promise, yet worded to keep hearts humble. \"Al-muflihun\" are those who succeed, the opposite of \"the wrongdoers will never succeed.\"\n\nThe verse sits after horror so that despair does not become another idol. Pharaoh's path is not the only path. The queen turned. Moses asked forgiveness. Scripture-believers said we already submitted. Success has a known recipe.\n\nA misconception is to take \"hope\" as doubt in Allah's mercy. The doubt is meant to fall on the servant's claim to have truly repented and worked. The next verse will recall that creating and choosing belong to the Lord, not to their pagan choosing of associates.",
  "68": "\"Your Lord creates and chooses whatever He wills—the choice is not theirs.\" Creation and choice, \"yakhluqu ma yasha'u wa yakhtar,\" are Allah's. \"Ma kana lahumul-khiyarah,\" they do not hold the choosing. They cannot appoint partners, or appoint who is prophet, or appoint which Book is true by taste.\n\nThis answers those who wanted the like of what Moses was given, or who chose idols as intercessors. Choosing in religion is not a human franchise.\n\n\"Glorified and Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!\" \"Subhan\" and \"ta'ala\" lift Him above the associates the Day will ask about. Praise here is a correction of theology.\n\nThe verse does not cancel human moral choice to repent or persist. It cancels their claim to share Allah's prerogatives. He creates. He chooses messengers and guidance's laws. They receive.\n\nThe next verse, already heard in the last chapter, repeats that the Lord knows what hearts conceal and reveal. Choice of His and knowledge of theirs close the net around hidden shirk and hidden repentance alike.",
  "69": "\"And your Lord knows what their hearts conceal and what they reveal.\" Repeated because the excuses of this passage—fear of being snatched, demand for Moses' miracles, desire, consultation of chiefs—have inner and outer faces. Some fears are spoken. Some pride is hidden. Both are known.\n\nThe hoopoe said the same of Allah who brings forth what is hidden. The last chapter told the Prophet his Lord knows chests. This chapter tells it again after \"the choice is not theirs.\" They cannot hide a private theology that differs from their public one.\n\nFor the repentant of the previous verse, this knowledge is comfort: a turned heart is seen even if people still accuse. For the arrogant, it is a threat: a smiling market face does not cover a partner in the chest.\n\nThe next verse gathers names and rights of Allah into one creed: no god except Him, praise in both lives, authority His, and the return to Him. Knowledge of secrets belongs to that One.",
  "70": "\"He is Allah. There is no god worthy of worship except Him.\" The core sentence of the whole Quran stands after stories of Pharaoh who claimed godhood and after a Day that asks where the associates are. \"La ilaha illa huwa\" is the repair of the land.\n\n\"All praise belongs to Him in this life and the next.\" Praise, \"al-hamd,\" is not only for gardens and night. It is His in both homes. The fleeting enjoyment of this life and the lasting good with Him both yield hamd to the same Lord.\n\n\"All authority is His.\" \"Al-hukm,\" rule and judgment. Pharaoh's hukm, the chiefs' hukm, the nine's oath, are borrowed shadows. Authority returns to its Owner, as the last chapter said He will judge between them by His justice.\n\n\"And to Him you will all be returned.\" The thought Pharaoh's army denied is stated as address to everyone. Return is the fact that makes arrogance \"with no right.\"\n\nThis verse is a small surah of creed inside the surah. The following questions about perpetual night and perpetual day will argue for this creed from the mercy of alternating light and rest.",
  "71": "\"Ask them, O Prophet, 'Imagine if Allah were to make the night perpetual for you until the Day of Judgment, which god other than Allah could bring you sunlight?'\" The mind is asked to stretch a known mercy until it breaks. If night never lifted until the Last Day, which \"ilah\" could bring \"diya',\" daylight?\n\nThe idols of Mecca cannot. Pharaoh cannot. The sun the people of Sheba worshipped cannot rise if the Lord of the sun holds the night. The question is unanswerable except by saying Allah.\n\n\"Will you not then listen?\" \"Tasma'un,\" will you hear. The previous chapter said he cannot make the deaf hear when they turn away. This question still invites hearing. The sign is simple enough for anyone who has waited for dawn.\n\nPerpetual night until the Day would also collapse the excuse of comfortable living. No market, no fruits brought to the sanctuary, no snatched land worth holding. The next verse flips the thought: perpetual day, and who could bring night to rest in.",
  "72": "\"Ask them also, 'Imagine if Allah were to make the day perpetual for you until the Day of Judgment, which god other than Allah could bring you night to rest in?'\" The twin of the last question. Endless day until the Hour, no shade of night, no sleep cycle. Who brings \"layl taskununa fih,\" night to settle in?\n\nThe last chapter said We made the night to rest in and the day bright. This chapter asks what if the pair were broken. The breaking reveals the Giver of the pair.\n\n\"Will you not then see?\" Hearing was asked after night. Seeing is asked after day. Both senses are summoned.\n\nA people who fear being snatched from land should fear being stuck in a land of unending glare with no rest. The gods they associate do not administer the switch. The next verse will state the positive: it is out of His mercy that He made day and night so you may rest and seek bounty and perhaps be grateful.",
  "73": "\"It is out of His mercy that He has made for you the day and night so that you may rest in the latter and seek His bounty in the former, and perhaps you will be grateful.\" Mercy, \"rahmah,\" is the reason for the alternation. Rest in night, seeking \"fadl\" in day—trade, farming, travel—are assigned to the two halves. The aim is \"tashkurun,\" that you may be grateful.\n\nSolomon's thanks and the many who do not thank return. The sanctuary's fruits, the safe haven, the night and day, are one mercy-system. Gratitude would be tawhid, worship of the Merciful who does this.\n\n\"For you\" makes the cosmos personal again. The perpetual-night and perpetual-day thoughts were terrors. This verse is the kindness those terrors were meant to uncover.\n\nThe next verse will repeat the Day's call about associate-gods. After mercy is spelled out so clearly, the question \"where are those you claimed\" becomes even heavier. They associated others while sleeping and trading under His mercy.",
  "74": "\"And watch for the Day He will call to them, 'Where are those you claimed were My associate-gods?'\" The call is repeated almost word for word. Repetition in the Quran is a teacher, not a slip. After a passage on night, day, rest, and bounty, the same question returns so the listener cannot file it far from daily mercy.\n\nThey claimed associates while using the day to seek fadl. The claim and the use did not match. The Day matches them by force.\n\nRepeating the call also structures the chapter's last movement: court scenes keep interrupting history so that Korah's coming story of treasure will not be heard as mere biography. Treasure, like night and day, will sit under the question of partners and proof.\n\nThe next verse adds witnesses from every community and a demand for proof, then the failure of fabricated gods. The repeated \"where\" is about to meet \"show Us your proof.\" ",
  "75": "\"And We will bring forth a witness from every faith-community and ask the polytheists, 'Show Us your proof.'\" From every ummah a witness, the prophet or a just testifier, is brought. Then the people of shirk are asked for \"burhan,\" proof, the same demand made in the last chapter: show me your proof if you are true.\n\n\"Then they will come to know that the truth is with Allah alone.\" Knowledge arrives late. \"Al-haqq\" is with Allah. Their system is seen as empty.\n\n\"And whatever gods they fabricated will fail them.\" \"Dalla 'anhum,\" what they invented strays from them, abandons them, goes missing. Fabrication, \"yaftarun,\" names the associates as made things, not found things.\n\nThe witness from each community means no nation is judged in a language it never heard. Moses will be there for his people, and the Prophet for his. The excuse of no warner is dead.\n\nAfter this court the chapter turns to Korah, a man from Moses' people who grew arrogant with keys of treasure. The theological argument becomes a story about wealth, the other idol that can stand beside stone gods.",
  "76": "\"Indeed, Korah was from the people of Moses, but he behaved arrogantly towards them.\" \"Qarun\" is of Israel, not of Pharaoh's blood. Oppression's tools can appear inside the rescued people. He \"bagha,\" acted insolently, against them. Victory at the sea did not purify every heart.\n\n\"We had granted him such treasures that even their keys would burden a group of strong men.\" The wealth is from \"We had granted.\" Keys alone need a strong troop, \"usbah uli al-quwwah.\" The picture is almost comic if it were not tragic: keys heavier than backs, and a soul proud of what the keys open.\n\n\"Some of his people advised him, 'Do not be prideful! Surely Allah does not like the prideful.'\" Counsel exists inside Israel as it existed at the well and in Sheba. They name the disease \"farah,\" exultant pride, and state a principle: Allah does not love \"al-farihin,\" those who swagger in joy of goods.\n\nKorah is a new Pharaoh of gold rather than of clay towers. Both elevate themselves in the land without right. The next verses will record his answer, the advice to seek the Hereafter without forgetting this world's lawful share, and his claim that he earned it by knowledge. The story is told so Mecca's rich, and every later rich believer, cannot say arrogance belongs only to unbelieving kings.",
  "77": "\"Rather, seek the reward of the Hereafter by means of what Allah has granted you\" is the advisers' better use of the keys. Wealth is a means, \"wabtaghi,\" seek with it the last home. The grant is admitted as Allah's, which already contradicts Korah's coming claim of knowledge.\n\n\"Without forgetting your share of this world\" stops a false monastic reading. \"Nasibaka min al-dunya\" is a lawful portion: food, family, needed goods. The Quran does not tell the rich man to throw the keys in a well. It tells him not to let the keys become a god.\n\n\"And be good to others as Allah has been good to you.\" Ihsan is measured by the ihsan already received. The treasures that burden strong men were goodness from Allah. The response is goodness to people, not a parade.\n\n\"Do not seek to spread corruption in the land, for Allah certainly does not like the corruptors.\" The last clause names Pharaoh's type again, \"al-mufsidin.\" Prideful wealth that crushes a people is ifsad in the land. Allah's dislike of corruptors is a refrain that should have frightened a man from Moses' own nation.\n\nThe advice is a complete economic ethic in a few lines: use goods for the Hereafter, take a just share, copy Allah's goodness, refuse corruption. Korah will answer with a theory of self-made knowledge. The contrast is the lesson.",
  "78": "\"He replied, 'I have been granted all this because of some knowledge I have.'\" The grant is rewritten as a wage of 'ilm. He thinks skill, craft, or esoteric know-how explains the keys. Thanks dies. The self becomes the source.\n\n\"Did he not know that Allah had already destroyed some from the generations before him who were far superior to him in power and greater in accumulating wealth?\" The Quran asks a knowledge question back. True knowledge would include history: stronger people, richer hoards, already destroyed. If knowledge produced safety, those generations would stand.\n\n\"There will be no need for the wicked to be asked about their sins.\" The wicked, \"al-mujrimun,\" will not be questioned as if the court lacks data, or their sins will be so obvious that interrogation is needless. Either reading removes Korah's hope that clever speech will audit well.\n\nThe man of keys fails the test of knowing. He knows how to gather. He does not know how others gathered more and still sank. The next verse shows him coming out in glamour, and the worldly wishers envying him, while people of knowledge speak a different wish.",
  "79": "\"Then he came out before his people in all his glamour.\" The advice is answered with a procession. \"Zinatihi,\" his adornment, the same word used for the decoration of this life. He makes the keys visible as clothing, servants, and show.\n\n\"Those who desired the life of this world wished, 'If only we could have something like what Korah has been given. He is truly a man of great fortune!'\" Desire, \"yuriduna al-hayat al-dunya,\" produces a dua of envy. They call him \"dhu hazzin azim,\" a man of huge luck. The fine promise of Allah is not in their sentence. Korah's fortune is.\n\nThe verse is dangerous because most listeners have felt that wish in a market. The Quran records it without yet swallowing the house, so we can recognize the wish as a spiritual risk.\n\nThe next verse will let those gifted with knowledge reply: shame on you, Allah's reward is better for those who believe and do good, and only the steadfast reach it. Two choruses after one parade: envy and knowledge.",
  "80": "\"But those gifted with knowledge said, 'Shame on you!'\" Knowledge speaks with a moral jolt, \"waylakum.\" The opposite of Korah's knowledge-claim is this gifted knowledge that fears Allah.\n\n\"Allah's reward is far better for those who believe and do good.\" The ranking of the last pages returns: what is with Allah is better and more lasting. Belief plus good work outranks keys that burden strong men.\n\n\"But none will attain this except the steadfast.\" \"Al-sabirun\" reach that reward. Patience is the price, the same sabr that earned a double wage earlier. Envying a parade is impatience of the heart.\n\nThese knowers stand in the line of the man who brought the throne in a blink by knowledge of the Scripture, and in the line of those who said we believe when the Quran was recited. Knowledge in this Book is a way of seeing value, not a way of justifying piles.\n\nThe next verse is the earth opening. Knowledge is about to be confirmed by an event the enviers cannot ignore.",
  "81": "\"Then We caused the earth to swallow him up, along with his home.\" \"Khasafna\" is a sinking, a swallowing. The man of keys and the house of keys go down together. Pharaoh was cast into the sea. Korah is cast into the ground. Water and earth both obey.\n\n\"There was no one to help him against Allah, nor could he even help himself.\" No troop of strong men who carried keys can lift the earth back. \"Ma kana lahu min fi'atin yansurunahu\" no clan helps him. He cannot help himself. The self that claimed knowledge is helpless.\n\nThe last chapter said on the Day they will not be helped. Korah receives a worldly preview. Associates, tribes, and treasures fail in one collapse.\n\nThe wishers of yesterday are about to change their sentence. The earth that swallowed him could have swallowed them. That near miss becomes theology on their tongues in the next verse: Allah gives abundant or limited provision, and disbelievers will never succeed.",
  "82": "\"And those who had craved his position the previous day began to say, 'Ah! It is certainly Allah Who gives abundant or limited provisions to whoever He wills of His servants.'\" Envy converts, at least in speech, when the house sinks. \"Basata\" and \"qadara,\" widening and tightening rizq, are Allah's. Korah's knowledge-theory dies with him.\n\n\"Had it not been for the grace of Allah, He could have surely caused the earth to swallow us up!\" They admit that desire for his place was almost a shared grave. \"Mann\" grace kept them above ground.\n\n\"Oh, indeed! The disbelievers will never succeed.\" They repeat Moses' principle about wrongdoers. Whether their hearts stay changed is not told. The verse shows how a sign can correct a slogan overnight.\n\nThe danger is a faith that only appears when someone else's villa sinks. Still, the corrected slogan is true and useful. The next verse will define who gets the Home of the Hereafter: those who do not seek tyranny or corruption, and the outcome of the righteous.",
  "83": "\"That eternal Home in the Hereafter We reserve only for those who seek neither tyranny nor corruption on the earth.\" \"Al-dar al-akhirah\" is assigned, \"naj'aluha,\" to a type of seeker: not those who want \"uluww,\" high-handedness, nor \"fasad,\" corruption. Pharaoh sought uluww. Korah's parade was uluww. Both spread fasad. The Home is not reserved for them.\n\n\"The ultimate outcome belongs only to the righteous.\" \"Al-aqibah lil-muttaqin,\" the end is for the mindful, those of taqwa. Moses said who will fare best in the end. This verse names them.\n\nThe enviers who almost wanted Korah's height are being taught what height is for. Reservation of the Home is Allah's choice, matching \"your Lord creates and chooses.\"\n\nThe next verse restates the payment law already heard at the end of the previous chapter: better for good, and only what they did for evil. Korah's story sits inside that law.",
  "84": "\"Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded with what is better.\" The chapter of the ant said this. This chapter says it again after a house sinks. Good is paid above its weight.\n\n\"And whoever comes with an evil deed, then the evildoers will only be rewarded for what they used to do.\" Evil is not multiplied as a cruelty. It is repaid as done. Korah used to do pride, parade, and a claim of self-knowledge. The earth answered that usage.\n\nRepetition of the law after two different stories, a queen's faith and a rich man's sinkhole, makes the law feel like the spine of the Book. Circumstances change. The scale does not.\n\nThe remaining verses turn from Korah to the Prophet's own road: the One who ordained the Quran will bring him home to Mecca, he must not side with disbelievers, not be turned from revelations, invite to his Lord, and not invoke any god with Allah. The stories have earned the right to command.",
  "85": "\"Most certainly, the One Who has ordained the Quran for you will ultimately bring you back home to Mecca.\" \"Farada 'alayka al-qur'an,\" imposed and destined the recitation for him. The same Lord will \"radd,\" return him to \"ma'ad,\" a place of return, understood by many commentators as Mecca, the home he would leave and re-enter. Other views include death or the Last Day. The English of this project specifies Mecca, which fits the chapter's long talk of the sacred city and being snatched from the land.\n\nThe promise answers the fear of Quraysh and the fear of the exiled Prophet. The land is Allah's to return people to.\n\n\"Say, 'My Lord knows best who has come with true guidance and who is clearly astray.'\" Moses said the first half to Pharaoh. The Prophet is given the same sentence. Two messengers, one theology of the end.\n\nIf the return is Mecca, later history fulfilled it in the conquest without the Prophet becoming Pharaoh. If the return is also the final return, the sentence still holds. The next verse recalls that he never expected the Book; it was a mercy, so he must not side with the disbelievers.",
  "86": "\"You never expected this Book to be revealed to you, but it came only as a mercy from your Lord.\" The Prophet did not scheme a career of scripture. \"Lam takun tarjoo,\" you were not hoping for it. The sending is \"rahmah,\" like his being sent as a mercy to warn a people without a warner.\n\n\"So never side with the disbelievers in their disbelief.\" Because the Book is mercy, alliance with those who deny it would be a betrayal of the gift. \"La takunannahum zahiran\" do not be a backer. Moses pledged not to side with the wicked after forgiveness. The Prophet is told not to side with kafirun after mercy.\n\nThe verse protects him in times of pressure, when Quraysh might offer compromise: worship our gods a year. Mercy does not negotiate its source.\n\nThe next verse will add: do not let them turn you from the revelations after they are sent down; invite to your Lord; do not be of the polytheists. Siding, turning away, and shirk are three related dangers.",
  "87": "\"Do not let them turn you away from the revelations of Allah after they have been sent down to you.\" The danger is being barred, \"yasuddunnaka,\" from ayat already given. Pressure comes after revelation, not only before. The Book on the tongue can still be pushed aside in practice if a messenger yielded. He is told not to yield.\n\n\"Rather, invite all to the Way of your Lord.\" The positive duty is \"ud'u,\" invite, to \"rabbik.\" Invitation replaces compromise. The Lord, not a blended cult, is the destination of the call.\n\n\"And never be one of the polytheists.\" The wording is strong. A prophet is told not to be of the mushrikin. If he must hear that, lesser callers must hear it. Shirk is not only statues. It is any sharing of the Lord's right that a social deal might ask.\n\nThe previous chapter ended with worship of the Lord of the sacred city and recitation. This verse is the same mission in defensive form: do not be turned, do invite, do not associate. The last verse of the chapter will make the creed explicit and close with perishability of all things except Allah, authority, and return.",
  "88": "\"And do not invoke any other god with Allah.\" Invocation, \"du'a,\" is worship. Calling another with Allah is the practical heart of shirk. The command is simple after long stories of Pharaoh, Haman, Korah, and Mecca's associates.\n\n\"There is no god worthy of worship except Him.\" The denial and affirmation sit together again.\n\n\"Everything is bound to perish except He Himself.\" \"Kullu shay'in halikun illa wajhahu,\" everything perishes except His Face, His Self. Keys, houses, seas, towers, sanctuaries as buildings, even the earth that swallows, all end. What does not perish is Allah.\n\n\"All authority belongs to Him. And to Him you will all be returned.\" The chapter's creed-verse is echoed as a seal. Hukm is His. Return is to Him. Korah could not help himself. Pharaoh's soldiers could not help him. Associates gave no response. The only remaining authority is the One who does not perish.\n\nSo the long narration that began with disconnected letters and a clear Book ends where a clear Book should: do not call another, nothing remains but He, and you are going back. The next chapter will open with other letters and a question about people who think saying \"we believe\" saves them from being tested."
};
